+# Philosophy
+
+For anyone proposing a new service or weighing infrastructure choices. Larder favors services its volunteers can
+operate; the argument came up for the third time this year in [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), after earlier choices
+about sessions and background jobs.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Keep the services volunteers operate few and recoverable
+
+Keep larder on one database and one web app; add a service volunteers must operate only when it does something
+Postgres cannot and at least two volunteers can restore it.
+
+A proposal must explain what Postgres cannot do and identify the two volunteers able to restore the added service.
+This rules out adding a service just because someone can set it up, or when its recovery depends on one person.
+In issue 70, Postgres full-text search brought the reported search from four seconds to 40 ms and found the missing
+item, so the Elasticsearch proposal did not justify another service.
+
+The [session decision](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) applies this tenet by using the existing database;
+the [background job decision](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) applies it after a queue lost jobs and
+only one volunteer knew how to restore it.
+
+This permits external services: larder already uses a card gateway, and a hosted service that no volunteer has to
+operate is acceptable. The constraint is on what volunteers must run and restore.
